  "text": "Online inference creates predictions at the time a request is served. However, llms are too large for online inference for tasks such as regression or classification. Consequently, engineering teams use offline inference instead of online inference. Offline inference is also called bulk inference and does not involve online inference; it saves computing resources and reduces consumption of computing resources while enabling use of larger llms. Slow prediction is acceptable for a task that runs only weekly or monthly. In practice, google search used an llm in offline inference to cache a list of more than 800 synonyms for Covid vaccines in more than 50 languages and to identify queries about vaccines using a cached list. Generally, llms learn from biases from the data used to train or distill the LLM. Therefore, llms should be used fairly according to fair use and should be used responsibly according to responsible use.",
